{
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication": "Warning",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection": "Warning",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore.HttpLogging": "Warning",
      "Microsoft.IdentityModel": "Warning",
      "System.Net.Http.HttpClient": "Warning"
    }
  },
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // ALLOWED HOSTS - THIS SERVICE ANSWERS ONLY UNDER THE NAMES IT IS REACHED BY
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // A SEMICOLON-DELIMITED ALLOW-LIST RATHER THAN `*`, AND IT IS DEFENCE IN DEPTH RATHER THAN THE
  // PRIMARY CONTROL. The discovery document this service publishes is composed entirely from
  // `Security:Issuer` and no longer reflects the request's scheme, host or path base, so a spoofed Host
  // header can no longer redirect a consumer's key fetch whatever this setting says. What the allow-list
  // adds is that a request naming an unexpected authority is refused outright instead of being served -
  // which matters for the service that holds the system's only signing key, and costs nothing to state.
  //
  // THE THREE ENTRIES ARE THE THREE NAMES A DEPLOYMENT ACTUALLY USES. `security-service` is the
  // container name every other service addresses this one by and is the authority of the issuer below;
  // `localhost` and `127.0.0.1` are the loopback names the local readiness probe and a development
  // bring-up use, and are the authority of the development issuer. An entry carries no port, which
  // matches any port on that authority - the port is fixed by the listener above, not here.
  //
  // A DEPLOYMENT REACHING THIS SERVICE UNDER ANOTHER NAME MUST EXTEND THIS LIST, and it already has to
  // set `Security:Issuer` to that name for any token to validate anywhere, so the two move together. A
  // request naming an authority outside the list is answered 400 by the host-filtering middleware before
  // any route runs.
  "AllowedHosts": "security-service;localhost;127.0.0.1",

  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // KESTREL - ONE LISTENER, ON THE ONE ENDPOINT KEY `Default`, AND IT IS THE SCHEME THE ATTACHED
  // ENVIRONMENT BINDS THIS SERVICE TO. Read this before changing either the key or the scheme.
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // WHY https, AND WHY ON THIS SERVICE THE TRANSPORT IS PART OF THE SECURITY ARGUMENT. This is the
  // system's trust bootstrap: the key set at /.well-known/jwks.json is what every other service
  // verifies against, and the body of a successful issuance response IS a credential. On a readable
  // channel the first is substitutable by anyone on path - an attacker who replaces it makes all three
  // verifiers accept tokens the attacker signed, each behaving exactly as designed - and the second is
  // replayable (CWE-319). The attached environment's `curl -sf http://localhost:5104/health` gate is a
  // probe SHAPE, restatable as `curl -sf --cacert <anchor> https://localhost:5104/health`, which is
  // what this image's HEALTHCHECK does; a substitutable trust bootstrap is not restatable at all. The
  // certificate comes from `Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Path` and `__KeyPath` in the deployment's
  // own secret layer, exactly as the signing key does, and Kestrel REFUSES TO START rather than
  // downgrading if it cannot resolve one. AAP 0.6.6.3 is about CALLER identity rather than the
  // channel: mutual TLS is the documented FALLBACK "for any pair where a token issuer is
  // inappropriate", which is a statement about credentials, not about encryption.
  //
  // WHAT AUTHENTICATES THE BOUNDARY, GIVEN THAT THE TRANSPORT DOES NOT (C-G). Every operation on
  // this service except the three named anonymous ones requires a bearer token, and `POST /v1/tokens`
  // - the one operation a bearer token cannot protect, because a caller cannot present a token in
  // order to obtain its first token - authenticates its caller against the `Security:Clients` roster
  // below. That roster is what keeps issuance callable on every topology without ever making it
  // anonymous - a header the operation reads itself needs no cooperation from whatever terminated the
  // handshake. A CLIENT CERTIFICATE IS THE OTHER ACCEPTED CREDENTIAL on that one operation, which is
  // the AAP's mutual-TLS fallback, AND THE LISTENER BELOW DECLARES IT RATHER THAN LEAVING IT TO A
  // DEPLOYMENT. `ClientCertificateMode: AllowCertificate` REQUESTS a certificate without demanding one,
  // so /health stays anonymously reachable and every bearer-authenticated operation is unaffected,
  // while the issuance operation can require one per route. Declaring it here is what makes the
  // certificate-identity path REACHABLE in the shipped environment: without it the handshake never asks
  // for a certificate, the ClientCertificateValidation callback Program.cs installs never runs, and
  // CallerCertificateTrust - a class with its own trust anchors, its own refusals and its own tests -
  // is dead code in production while every document describing it still reads as live. `RequireCertificate`
  // is the value NOT used, deliberately: it aborts the handshake for a client presenting none, which
  // would take the anonymous /health probe with it and the readiness chain gating Gateway could never
  // open. The server certificate and key come from Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Path and __KeyPath
  // in the deployment's own secret layer, and the anchor callers are checked against comes from the
  // internal-TLS trust setting - no path, password or thumbprint appears here (C-F).
  //
  // WHAT PLAINTEXT WOULD HAVE COST, WHICH IS WHY NONE IS DECLARED. On a cleartext listener the
  // published key set at /.well-known/jwks.json is substitutable by anyone on path and a bearer token
  // is replayable by anyone on path. Neither exposure is accepted anywhere in this estate; both are
  // recorded here so that a later deployment cannot reintroduce cleartext as a convenience without
  // knowing what it is trading away.
  //
  // WHY Kestrel:Endpoints AND NOT A TOP-LEVEL `Urls` KEY. `Urls` carries addresses with no protocol
  // association, and this endpoint names a PROTOCOL SET that a bare address cannot express. The value
  // is "Http1" rather than "Http1AndHttp2", and that is deliberate: this service is REST-only, and a
  // listener that accepts only what it is for cannot be misaddressed silently. Naming both would
  // advertise a version nothing here serves.
  // This service is REST-only - it publishes no gRPC contract - so nothing here needs HTTP/2 at all,
  // which is why it binds ONE endpoint where Persistence and DataServices each bind two. The endpoint
  // form also keeps one place for a deployment to add TLS settings without moving the address.
  //
  // ONE ENDPOINT KEY, IN EVERY ENVIRONMENT, AND THE OVERLAY OVERRIDES IT RATHER THAN ADDING A SECOND.
  // Kestrel binds every endpoint it finds under Kestrel:Endpoints, and configuration MERGES by key -
  // so a Development overlay that introduced a second key at the same address would leave BOTH
  // present and the host would fail to start with an address already in use. appsettings.
  // Development.json therefore overrides `Default` by name. Do not rename either side in isolation.
  // The documented local readiness probe consequently becomes
  // `curl -sf --cacert <anchor> https://localhost:5104/health`.
  "Kestrel": {
    "Endpoints": {
      "Default": {
        "Url": "https://+:5104",
        "Protocols": "Http1",
        "ClientCertificateMode": "AllowCertificate"
      }
    }
  },

  // THERE IS NO RequireHttpsMetadata LEAF IN THIS SECTION, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN AN
  // OMISSION. It would be read by nothing. The setting governs whether the bearer handler may fetch
  // discovery metadata over cleartext, and it is consulted only when an Authority or a MetadataAddress
  // is configured - which this service configures NEITHER of, because it validates the tokens it
  // minted itself and takes its verification key in process from its own signing-key layer. With no
  // metadata retrieval there is no fetch for the setting to govern. Carrying the key anyway would tell
  // an operator that Security's metadata retrieval is held to HTTPS when Security performs none, which
  // is a false assurance. The setting IS live and IS set on the three services that genuinely fetch
  // this service's key set - see their own appsettings.json - and nothing here relaxes it there.
  "Authentication": {
    "Jwt": {
      "Audience": "powerframework-security",
      "ValidateIssuer": true,
      "ValidateAudience": true,
      "ValidateLifetime": true,
      "ValidateIssuerSigningKey": true,
      "MapInboundClaims": false
    }
  },
  "Security": {
    "Issuer": "https://security-service:5104",
    "Audiences": [
      "powerframework-gateway",
      "powerframework-dataservices",
      "powerframework-persistence",
      "powerframework-security"
    ],

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE ISSUANCE PERMISSION MATRIX - WHO MAY ASK FOR WHAT.
    //
    // The roster above says which audiences EXIST. This says who may address them, and it is what turns
    // an authenticated caller into an authorised one. Without it, any caller whose certificate chained
    // to Security:MutualTls:ClientCaPath could mint a token for ANY service in the system carrying ANY
    // scope set it named, and every requested scope was granted verbatim - a confused deputy in the
    // middle of the token topology, and the opposite of what a sole issuer is for (CWE-862, CWE-863).
    //
    // ONE GRANT PER (CALLER, AUDIENCE) PAIR, which is what makes this a matrix rather than two lists.
    // DataServices addresses two audiences with entirely different scope sets; a flat per-caller scope
    // list would let it carry Security's cryptographic scope in a Persistence-audience token.
    //
    // AN AUDIENCE IS REFUSED, A SCOPE SET IS INTERSECTED. A request naming an audience this caller has
    // no grant for is answered 403 - the same 403 as an audience this issuer does not serve at all, so
    // the response cannot be used to enumerate the roster. A scope set is granted as the OVERLAP with
    // the grant below and the granted set is reported in the response, which is the published contract's
    // own rule: the granted set may be narrower than the requested one, a narrowing is a success rather
    // than an error, and an empty granted set is a legal response meaning nothing requested was granted.
    //
    // EVERY VALUE HERE IS A NAME. No certificate, no key, no thumbprint, no secret - the CHAIN
    // establishes who a caller is, and this only records what that identity permits (constraint C-F).
    //
    // THE THREE GRANTS BELOW ARE EXACTLY THE CALL GRAPH THE SERVICES IMPLEMENT, and no more. The
    // topology is layered and acyclic: Gateway reaches DataServices, DataServices reaches Persistence
    // and Security, and nothing else calls anything. A deployment that additionally wants an operator to
    // reach an authenticated /v1/ping route adds that operator's certificate identity here with a grant
    // for the service it is pinging; nothing is pre-granted for it, because an unused permission is
    // still a permission.
    //
    // WHICH HAS ONE CONSEQUENCE WORTH STATING OUTRIGHT RATHER THAN LEAVING TO BE DISCOVERED AT A 401.
    // `powerframework-gateway` appears in the audience roster above and in NO grant below as an
    // audience, because nothing inside the system calls the gateway - it is the ingress. So out of the
    // box no caller can obtain a token addressed to it, and every authenticated gateway route,
    // /v1/ping and the /v1/datawindow/** projections alike, answers 401 to every credential this
    // issuer will mint. That is fail-closed on purpose: pre-granting an invented external-client
    // identity would ship a live permission for a caller nobody had asked for.
    //
    // TO OPEN THE INGRESS, add the external client's own certificate identity as a caller below with a
    // grant whose audience is `powerframework-gateway`. The scope list on that grant may be anything
    // the deployment finds useful for its own auditing, INCLUDING a single placeholder, because the
    // gateway itself requires no scope: its published contract applies the bearer scheme with an empty
    // scope array to every operation and attributes its 403 to a projected `PermissionDenied` raised
    // downstream, so the scope decision belongs to DataServices and Persistence and is enforced there.
    // The grant needs at least one scope only because a grant listing none is refused at startup.
    //
    // TWO AUTHORING SHAPES EXIST FOR THIS ONE DECISION, AND THIS FILE DELIBERATELY USES ONLY ONE.
    // The issuer reads the UNION of `Security:CallerAuthorizations` - one flat Caller / Audience / Scopes
    // row per pair, used below - and `Security:Callers`, which nests each caller's grants under its
    // identity. Both bind, both are validated entry by entry, and both are enforced through the same
    // matrix, so a deployment may author either. THIS FILE STATES THE MATRIX EXACTLY ONCE, in the flat
    // shape, for two reasons that are not stylistic:
    //
    //   * STATING IT TWICE WOULD MAKE A STALE EDIT INVISIBLE. Because the two shapes UNION rather than
    //     collide, an operator who tightened one and forgot the other would leave the looser statement in
    //     force with nothing refused and nothing logged. One statement cannot go stale against itself.
    //   * THE ORCHESTRATION TEMPLATE EXTENDS THIS ARRAY BY INDEX. orchestration/.env.example adds the
    //     end-to-end suite's grant through `Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__*`, and the configuration
    //     provider merges an array by INDEX rather than by appending - so the three rows below occupy
    //     indices 0, 1 and 2, and their COUNT AND ORDER are load-bearing. A fourth row added here would be
    //     wholly shadowed by that block, silently. If a row is legitimately added, move the template's
    //     index to match.
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    "CallerAuthorizations": [
      {
        "Caller": "powerframework-gateway",
        "Audience": "powerframework-dataservices",
        "Scopes": [ "dataservices.datawindow", "dataservices.columnexpression" ]
      },
      {
        "Caller": "powerframework-dataservices",
        "Audience": "powerframework-persistence",
        "Scopes": [ "persistence.read", "persistence.write" ]
      },
      {
        "Caller": "powerframework-dataservices",
        "Audience": "powerframework-security",
        "Scopes": [ "security.crypto", "ping" ]
      }
    ],

    // THE ISSUANCE ROSTER. WHO MAY OBTAIN A TOKEN, AND WHAT EACH MAY ASK FOR.
    //
    // Audiences above says which audiences this DEPLOYMENT serves - one answer for every caller. On its
    // own that authorises any caller able to authenticate at the issuance edge to mint for ANY of those
    // audiences carrying ANY scope set it chose to name, which is not least privilege and is not what a
    // roster of service identities looks like to a reader. This section is the per-caller statement, and
    // the issuer consults both gates on every request: deployment-wide first, then the entry below.
    //
    // NAMES ONLY, AND SecretConfigurationKey IS A NAME TOO. The value it points at is a FLAT
    // configuration key supplied by the orchestration secret layer - generate one per caller with
    // `openssl rand -base64 32`. No secret appears here, in any other settings file, or in any container
    // definition, which is the same indirection KeyStore uses and the discipline the repository-wide
    // secrets mandate requires. An entry may omit the member entirely: such a caller authenticates by
    // CLIENT CERTIFICATE only, which is what a TLS-terminating deployment uses. A member that is PRESENT
    // and names a key resolving to nothing REFUSES THE HOST, because the alternative is a caller that
    // can never authenticate against a service reporting healthy.
    //
    // EVERY GRANT BELOW IS EVIDENCED BY A CALL SITE IN THIS REPOSITORY rather than chosen for
    // convenience, which is what keeps the roster auditable:
    //   * powerframework-gateway asks Security for a token addressed to DataServices carrying
    //     dataservices.datawindow and dataservices.columnexpression
    //     [services/gateway-service/PowerFramework.Gateway/Clients/DataServicesClient.cs:1286-1308].
    //   * powerframework-dataservices asks for security.crypto against this service
    //     [services/dataservices-service/PowerFramework.DataServices/Clients/SecurityClient.cs:1954-2051]
    //     and for persistence.read plus persistence.write against Persistence
    //     [services/dataservices-service/PowerFramework.DataServices/Clients/PersistenceClient.cs:424-461].
    //   * The `ping` scope is what THIS service's own authenticated probe requires
    //     [Endpoints/PingEndpoints.cs], and the (powerframework-dataservices -> powerframework-security)
    //     row below carries it so that probe is reachable at all. IT WAS ABSENT, AND THE CONSEQUENCE WAS
    //     NOT A NARROWER PERMISSION BUT AN UNUSABLE ENDPOINT: the route requires `ping` for audience
    //     `powerframework-security`, no row granted that pair, so every attempt either failed to mint
    //     (unlisted pair) or minted without the scope and met a 403. An authenticated probe that cannot
    //     succeed under the configuration it ships with is not a probe. The scope is added to the row
    //     for the caller that already declares it AND already addresses this audience - see Clients
    //     below - rather than to a new row, so the row COUNT AND ORDER are untouched and the
    //     index-merge hazard recorded above is not disturbed.
    //   * THE OTHER TWO ROWS ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT WITHOUT `ping`. This matrix grants a capability only
    //     where a caller exercises it, and nothing in this repository calls Gateway's or DataServices'
    //     `/v1/ping` from another service - the readiness path between services is the ANONYMOUS
    //     `/health`. Widening those two rows would grant a credential nothing asks for, which is the
    //     same rule that keeps Persistence out of this matrix entirely.
    // A grant nothing calls for is deliberately absent: Persistence requests no token at all - it reads
    // this service's published key set anonymously - so it has NO ENTRY HERE, and adding one would grant
    // a credential nothing asks for.
    //
    // THE OPERATOR / END-TO-END IDENTITY IS NOT DECLARED HERE. It lives in
    // appsettings.Development.json, because a suite identity is a local-bring-up fact rather than a
    // deployment one and a production roster should not carry a caller that only a test presents.
    "Clients": [
      {
        "Subject": "powerframework-gateway",
        "SecretConfigurationKey": "SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET_GATEWAY",
        "Audiences": [
          "powerframework-dataservices",
          "powerframework-security"
        ],
        "Scopes": [
          "dataservices.datawindow",
          "dataservices.columnexpression",
          "ping"
        ]
      },
      {
        "Subject": "powerframework-dataservices",
        "SecretConfigurationKey": "SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET_DATASERVICES",
        "Audiences": [
          "powerframework-persistence",
          "powerframework-security"
        ],
        "Scopes": [
          "persistence.read",
          "persistence.write",
          "security.crypto",
          "ping"
        ]
      }
    ],

    "TokenLifetime": "00:05:00",
    "SigningAlgorithm": "RS256",

    // SigningKeyFormat AND SigningKeyMinimumSizeBits ARE BOTH BOUND OPTIONS AND BOTH ARE ENFORCED.
    // CHANGING EITHER VALUE CHANGES BEHAVIOUR.
    //
    // ⚠ AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS BLOCK SAID THE OPPOSITE, IN CAPITALS, AND WAS WRONG ⚠
    //
    // It stated that Configuration/SecurityOptions.cs "declares no SigningKeyFormat property and no
    // SigningKeyMinimumSizeBits property, so the two leaves below bind to nothing", that changing either
    // "changes no behaviour whatsoever", and - most consequentially - that a 1024-bit key "starts the host
    // and mints tokens". All three claims are false against the delivered code. It even named its own
    // remediation: "Whoever adds a real floor here must add the bound option, the validator and the test
    // together, and must update docs/SECRETS.md section 4.1 and docs/BUILD.md section 8 in the same edit."
    // The floor was added; that documentation half was not, and this correction is it. The direction of
    // the error is what made it worth a finding rather than a typo: an operator reading it was told an
    // active control was inert, so they would have expected a short key to be accepted and read the
    // startup refusal as a defect - and anyone auditing the service would have recorded a weakness that
    // does not exist. NOTHING ABOUT THE BEHAVIOUR CHANGED IN THIS EDIT; only the description did (AAP G2).
    //
    // What each leaf actually is:
    //
    //   * SigningKeyFormat is BOUND (SecurityOptions.SigningKeyFormat) and VALIDATED
    //     (SecurityOptionsValidator.CheckSigningMaterial, via SigningKeyFormats.IsRecognised). The
    //     recognised set has exactly one member - the value below - so a deployment naming anything else
    //     is REFUSED AT STARTUP by name rather than ignored. It remains a description of a fixed
    //     acceptance sequence in the sense that Tokens/SigningKeyProvider.cs always attempts the same
    //     closed sequence; what changed is that a value which disagrees with that sequence is now a
    //     startup failure instead of a silently-ignored leaf.
    //
    //   * SigningKeyMinimumSizeBits is BOUND (SecurityOptions.SigningKeyMinimumSizeBits, default 2048)
    //     AND IS A REAL, ENFORCED FLOOR, measured in TWO places deliberately:
    //       - SecurityOptionsValidator.CheckSigningMaterial imports the key, measures the modulus and
    //         refuses a short one with a named configuration failure - which is what produces a readable
    //         startup message; and
    //       - Tokens/SigningKeyProvider calls RequireSufficientModulus before signing credentials become
    //         reachable at all - which is what makes the guarantee structural rather than dependent on
    //         options validation being in the path.
    //     A 1024-bit RSA key therefore does NOT start this host: it is refused, by name. The floor may be
    //     raised (3072, 4096); it may not be lowered below AbsoluteMinimumSigningKeySizeBits = 2048, and a
    //     configured value beneath that is itself refused. This leaf IS a control and may be cited as one.
    //
    // SECURITY_JWT_SIGNING_KEY is a VALUE rather than a path, and it arrives from an environment file.
    // "PemOrPkcs8Base64" names the ACCEPTANCE ORDER and the closed set of shapes: PEM is
    // tried first - both the PKCS#8 form `openssl genpkey ... -out key.pem` emits and the older PKCS#1
    // form an existing key may already be in - and base64 of the DER encoding of a PKCS#8 structure on one
    // line is the fallback. That single-line form is what `openssl genpkey ... -outform DER | base64 -w0`
    // produces and what orchestration/.env.example instructs, because this file format has no line
    // continuation so a multi-line PEM block cannot be expressed in the template at all; PEM stays
    // accepted for the deployment path where the value arrives from a secret store that can carry
    // newlines. NEITHER SHAPE MAY BE REFUSED: the legacy generator's PEM output is an OPTIONAL fourth
    // argument [ws_objects/pfw.crypto.pbl.src/n_crypto.sru:L19-L20], so legacy private-key material
    // genuinely exists in both. Anything that is neither - random bytes from `openssl rand` being the
    // case that has actually happened - fails the import and the host refuses to start.
    //
    // WHY 2048 IS IMPOSED HERE AND DELIBERATELY NOT IMPOSED ON THE C-02 SURFACE. This key is the system's
    // OWN signing identity - the trust root every verifier validates against - and it is NET-NEW: the
    // legacy has no token issuer at all, so a floor on it preserves no legacy behaviour and violates none
    // either, which is exactly what makes it a legitimate control rather than a correction of the legacy
    // (AAP G2, 0.6.6.4).
    //
    // THE LEGACY'S 1024-BIT ALLOWANCE IS UNTOUCHED WHERE IT IS THE LEGACY'S. The catalogue keeps
    // CRYPTO_RSA_BITS_1024 = 1024 a first-class value [ws_objects/pfw.shared.pbl.src/enums.sru:L965], and
    // that weakness is reproduced verbatim on C-02's key-generation surface, where the CALLER supplies the
    // size and parity is the obligation (see Crypto/LegacyDefaults.cs). The floor above applies to this
    // service's own signing identity and to nothing else - one surface preserves the legacy, the other
    // protects a net-new trust root, and conflating them would either weaken the issuer or break parity.
    //
    // KEEPING THE THREE STATEMENTS IN STEP. docs/SECRETS.md section 4.1 and docs/BUILD.md section 8 state
    // the same thing as this block. They were all three wrong together once, in the same direction, so
    // whoever changes the floor, its default, or its absolute minimum must change all four places - those
    // two documents, this block, and orchestration/.env.example - in one edit.
    "SigningKeyFormat": "PemOrPkcs8Base64",
    "SigningKeyMinimumSizeBits": 2048,

    "SigningKeyId": "powerframework-security-signing-1",
    "TokenEndpointPath": "/v1/tokens",
    "JwksPath": "/.well-known/jwks.json",
    "OpenIdConfigurationPath": "/.well-known/openid-configuration",

    // The client-certificate trust anchor, and how thoroughly a presented certificate's revocation is
    // checked. Declared here with their defaults rather than left to the options type, so that this file
    // remains the complete picture of the Security section: a member that exists in configuration but
    // nowhere in the settings file is one an operator cannot discover, and the orchestration template
    // documents both of these by name.
    //
    // AN EMPTY ANCHOR MEANS NO CLIENT-CERTIFICATE RECONCILIATION IS PERFORMED, which is the default
    // posture because the estate's default is JWT: mutual TLS is the documented per-pair fallback, not
    // the standing mechanism, so a deployment that wants it states the anchor itself. Supplying an
    // anchor is what turns the check on, and from that point it FAILS CLOSED - chain, validity window
    // and revocation are all established before any common-name reconciliation is attempted.
    //
    // NoCheck is the default revocation mode, and it is a deliberate default rather than an oversight:
    // an air-gapped or offline deployment has no distribution point to reach, and a mode that could not
    // be satisfied would turn every caller into a refusal. Offline and Online are the two stricter
    // values, and a deployment with reachable revocation data should name one.
    "ClientCertificateAuthorityPath": "",
    "ClientCertificateRevocationMode": "NoCheck",

    "KeyStore": {
      "ConfigurationKeyPrefix": "SECURITY_KEYSTORE_",
      "PermittedKeyRefs": []
    },

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // WHOSE CLIENT CERTIFICATES THIS SERVICE ACCEPTS ON POST /v1/tokens
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // THE MISSING HALF OF THE MUTUAL-TLS EDGE, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS A REAL VULNERABILITY RATHER THAN
    // an incomplete convenience. Token issuance derives the caller's identity from the presented
    // certificate's COMMON NAME and reconciles it against the claimed subject. A name proves nothing
    // unless the chain behind it is verified: with no anchor anywhere, a caller could present a
    // self-signed certificate whose common name is `powerframework-gateway` and be minted a Gateway
    // token for every audience Gateway is permitted. The documented topology issues caller
    // certificates from a LOCAL authority that is in no container's operating-system trust store, so
    // the platform could not make that decision either.
    //
    // ONE PATH, AND DELIBERATELY NO KEY PATH. Verifying a chain needs only the public root, and an
    // anchor with its private key beside it would let THIS service issue the very caller identities
    // it authenticates - so a compromise here would become a compromise of every caller rather than
    // of the signing key alone.
    //
    // EMPTY IS A VALID STATE and is the default: it means the platform's own trust store decides,
    // which is correct where caller certificates come from an authority the image already trusts, and
    // is what a test host relying on a directly supplied certificate needs. It is NOT a bypass - the
    // platform's validation still runs first, so a certificate it rejects never reaches the handler.
    // Supplied by SECURITY_MTLS_CLIENT_CA_PATH in orchestration/.env.example. A path that is set but
    // unreadable is a structural fault and the host refuses to start rather than accept callers on an
    // unverified name.
    "MutualTls": {
      "ClientCaPath": ""
    }
  }
}
